WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.160
24 June 1914.

2
00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:07.820
An express train pulls into a
tiny country station, and stops.

3
00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:09.300
It isn't meant to.

4
00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:11.120
"The steam hissed.

5
00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:13.190
Someone cleared his throat."

6
00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.140
Then, nothing.

7
00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:21.030
In January 1915, a poet begins
to write about that minute: "Yes.

8
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.420
I remember Adlestrop" A train stops.

9
00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:26.650
Nobody gets on or off.

10
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:29.130
So why would a poem remember it?

11
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.390
In this mission, you'll
listen to the short,

12
00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.190
still sentences that
make time almost stop.

13
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.740
You'll see how its four
quatrains are built,

14
00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.200
rhyme by rhyme.

15
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.790
And you'll follow where the
speaker's attention goes,

16
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.650
once the train is still.

17
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.880
Your mission: explain why a
poem remembers one minute.
